    "knowledge_point": "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle",
    "problem": "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ are solutions to the equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}-y=0$ for $x>0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?",
    "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}\\frac{1}{x}+{c}_{2}\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+\\sqrt{x}=\\frac{2+x\\sqrt{x}}{x}\n$$",
